ater  aspirations I never imagined that I  would be serving in the United States  especially through a number of  challenges that I've been through in my  lifetime but I am so grateful and the  title of my book says it all it's the my  ode to the country that really means we  are  so blessed to be in such a tremendous  nation where you can be a farm girl  growing up in rural Iowa and then serve  your nation in uniform as well as serve  your constituents and in the Senate  senator senator not just a farm girl but  a farm girl that is castrated hogs when  she was young who wore plastic bag on  her shoes  to save her shoes you talk about growing  up as a farmer's daughter you said it  was excellent training you said for  challenges you faced in military and now  in public office your tagline now reads  mother soldier senator go you so how was  how was the way you raised affected  affected you and and and really gave  good examples for you when you had  military life in public office because  you do draw a correlation between the  two yes and thank you Gail because I  grew up with a mom and dad that really  believed you had to pitch in and work  and a lot of that was manual labor  that's what farming entails so not being  afraid to get my hands dirty  understanding there is no job beneath me  and that really instilled the values and  the work ethic that have carried me on  through the military challenging myself  in various situations and knowing that I  can overcome and of course always  circling back to gratitude knowing that  I couldn't have done all of this without  wonderful friends and family supporting  me yeah you know and Senator listen  you've had a lot to overcome you write  very candidly I have to say very  pointedly about abuse you suffered from  a boyfriend at a very early age then  later in your marriage abuse and  infidelity and you said all of those  certainly changed you but what is your  message to women who have gone through  it or who are going through it now you  have a very clear point of view on this  yeah my story is a very raw story but  it's not a story that is unusual across  the United States and I've had many  women that have thanked me for being so  candid about whether it's sexual or  physical abuse and my my  point to other women and other young  ladies out there is that this is what a  mom can be this is what a mom can do she  can work she can serve in the military  she can serve in elected office she can  raise a family there are so many things  and opportunities we just have to know  how to pick ourselves up after having  those challenging situations drive  through it all and know that we can be  all of these things we can be  aspirational and generational yes and  you said you know you you you think  about your daughter  20 year old Libby I'm so glad that you  have her in your life as someone who you  said suffered from low self-esteem and  low self-worth you certainly turned that  around senator erst we thank you thank  you and good luck with your book it's  called daughter of the Hartland  it's on sale now wherever you like to  buy your book
 

